## UI Components

### 1. Queue icon on YouTube thumbnails

Each YouTube video card gets a new "Queue" icon button, behaving similarly to the existing archive X button:

- **Icon style:** playlist-style icon (stacked horizontal lines with a play triangle, similar to YouTube's playlist icon). Use SVG for crispness and theme support.
- **Default state (video NOT in queue):**
  - Icon is HIDDEN by default
  - Appears on hover over the thumbnail (mirroring the existing archive X button behavior)
  - Dark/neutral background with white icon
  - Click → adds the video to the queue
- **Active state (video IS in queue):**
  - Icon is VISIBLE AT ALL TIMES (not just on hover)
  - Background is GREEN
  - Instead of the queue icon, a WHITE NUMBER is displayed showing the video's position in the queue (1, 2, 3...)
  - Click → removes the video from the queue. All other queued videos renumber accordingly.
- **Position:** Direct child of `.content-card` (same DOM layer as the existing `.archive-btn`), positioned absolute on the right side of the card, **below** the archive button with a small gap between them (e.g. `top: 44px; right: 8px` — the archive button sits at `top: 8px; right: 8px`). The gap is intentional to reduce the chance of accidentally clicking one when meaning to click the other. Do NOT place inside `.thumbnail-container`.
- **Dark mode:** both states must support light and dark themes.

### 2. Queue badge in YouTube section header

A new badge in the YouTube section header.

- **Location:** A new flex element in the YouTube section header row, inserted between the `<h2>` title element and the `.cache-info` div (which contains "Last updated" and "Refreshes"). The section header uses `display: flex; justify-content: space-between`, so adding a new sibling element naturally slots it into the row.
- **Visibility:** only appears when the queue has at least one video. Hidden when queue is empty.
- **Content:** `Queue (N) ▶` where N is the queue count.
- **Two clickable zones:**
  - The **play button (▶ portion)** — clicking launches the player directly without opening the modal.
  - The **rest of the badge** ("Queue (N)" text) — clicking opens the queue modal.
- **Dark mode:** must support both themes.

### 3. Queue modal

Opens when the user clicks the non-play portion of the Queue badge.

**Purpose:** lets the user view, reorder, and edit the queue without starting the player. Useful while building a queue.

**Contents:**
- Modal header with title "Queue" and a close X
- A "Play" button at the top of the modal (launches the player)
- The list of queued videos:
  - Thumbnail (small)
  - Title
  - Channel name
  - Position number
  - Watched videos appear grayed out
  - Drag handles for reorder
  - Individual remove (X) button per item
  - "Watch Again" button (with eye icon) appears on watched/grayed items only — clicking toggles the item back to unwatched in its current position
- A "Clear All" button at the bottom
- Standard modal close behavior (X, escape key, click outside)

### 4. Player section

Appears when the user starts the player. Pushes other dashboard content down (new section, not a modal overlay).

**Contents:**
- Embedded YouTube IFrame player, responsive 16:9
- Below the player:
  - Current video title
  - Channel name
  - Position indicator ("Video 2 of 5")
  - Control buttons: **Previous**, **Next**, **Archive** (marks current video as watched — see two-stage model), **Close** (X)
- Below the controls, a **Queue panel** (see next section)

### 5. Queue panel (below player)

Visible whenever the player is active, regardless of play/pause state. Functions like YouTube's "Up Next" panel.

**Contents:**
- Same queue list as in the modal: thumbnails, titles, position numbers, drag handles, individual remove buttons, Watch Again buttons on watched items, Clear All button.
- **Currently playing video** is highlighted with:
  - A subtle glow around the outside of the item
  - Changed background color for that row
- Watched (grayed) videos remain functional for reordering and removal — graying is purely visual.
- Stays in sync with the modal (same underlying data).

