## Behavior & Logic

### Adding to queue
- Click queue icon on a thumbnail (when not in queue) → adds video to end of queue array.
- All open queue views (modal, panel) update immediately.
- The Queue badge appears in the YouTube section header if it wasn't already visible.

### Removing from queue
- Click queue icon on a thumbnail (when in queue) → removes from queue.
- Click X on a queue item in the modal or panel → removes from queue.
- All views update immediately. Position numbers on remaining items renumber.
- If queue becomes empty AND player is not active, the Queue badge disappears.

### Reordering
- Drag-and-drop in the modal or panel.
- All views update immediately.
- Position numbers on thumbnails update.

### Watch Again (on watched/grayed items)
- Toggles the item back to `watched: false`.
- Keeps the item in its current position in the queue (does NOT move it to "play next").
- Gray styling is removed.
- If the user wants it to play next, they can drag it manually.

### Starting the player
- Triggered by:
  - Clicking the play button (▶) inside the Queue badge
  - Clicking the "Play" button inside the Queue modal
- Player section appears and pushes other content down.
- First unwatched video in the queue starts playing.
- If all videos in queue are already watched, see "Queue complete" section below.

### Playback tracking
- Use YouTube IFrame Player API.
- Poll current playback time periodically (every ~1 second is fine).
- Track **actual playback time** — not playhead position. This means we accumulate watched seconds only when the video is in PLAYING state. If the user scrubs forward, the scrubbed portion does NOT count.
- When accumulated watched time ≥ 90% of `durationSeconds`, mark the video as `watched: true`.
- Visual update: the video grays out in all queue views.

**Zero-duration videos (`durationSeconds === 0`):** In practice, zero duration means one of two things: (1) a scheduled/premiere video that hasn't gone live yet — the player won't play it, so no tracking occurs and it sits inert in the queue; or (2) a live stream currently in progress — the player can play it but the total duration is unknown. For both cases, skip the 90% threshold entirely and rely solely on the IFrame API `ended` event to mark the video as `watched: true`. For case (1), `ended` never fires so the video is never auto-marked watched (correct). For case (2), `ended` fires when the stream concludes (reasonable). The user can always use the manual **Archive** button in the player to mark a zero-duration video watched at any time.

### Auto-advance
- When the current video ends (IFrame API "ended" event), automatically load the next UNWATCHED video in the queue.
- "Next unwatched" means: scan the queue in order from the current position, find the next item with `watched: false`.
- If no more unwatched videos remain, trigger the "Queue complete" flow.

### Manual controls
- **Next** button: skip to the next unwatched video. Does NOT mark current as watched (unless 90% was already reached).
- **Previous** button: jump back to the previous video in the queue (whether watched or not — user can re-watch).
- **Archive** button: marks the current video as watched immediately (sets `watched: true`). Does not automatically advance to next — user can keep watching if they want.
- **Close (X)** button: triggers session-end logic (see below).

### Removing the currently-playing video
- Allowed.
- Player advances to the next unwatched video.
- The removed video disappears from the queue.
- If the removed video was the last one, triggers "Queue complete" flow.

